<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Library validation loads a library only when its team identifier
         matches the signing process, and Apple issues that identifier. An
         ad-hoc signature has none - and 2026-09-19 established that a
         self-signed certificate has none either, whatever its subject says:
         the packaged app died in dyld with "mapping process and mapped file
         (non-platform) have different Team IDs" while loading its own Electron
         framework, before running a line of its own code.
         Every macOS signature this repository can produce is one of those two,
         so they all use this file. It buys nothing back that was ever there:
         without a team identifier, library validation cannot tell our own
         framework from anyone else's, so its only effect was to stop the app
         from starting. The whole bundle is still sealed by the signature, and
         the rest of the hardened runtime still applies.
         build/entitlements.mac.plist keeps library validation on and is used
         the day a Developer ID signature exists; this exception is withdrawn
         in the same change. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
